<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaʻahumanu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1768">1768</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Kaahumanu, Elizabeth Kaʻahumanu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaʻahumanu was the favourite and most politically powerful wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> and, as kuhina nui, co-ruler of the Hawaiian Kingdom under his first two successors. Born at Hāna on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, she married Kamehameha at thirteen. After his death in 1819 she shared power with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-ii">Kamehameha II</a> and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keopuolani">Keōpūolani</a> and the high priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hewahewa">Hewahewa</a>, brought about the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a>. In 1821 she and Kamehameha II had Kaumualiʻi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> kidnapped, and she married him by force. She converted to Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 1824, ordered the first Catholic missionaries to leave in 1827, banned the hula in 1830, and died near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> in 1832.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu.jpg" alt="Kaʻahumanu" width="330" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Choris / Jean-Pierre Norblin de La Gourdaine · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaahumanu_with_servant.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1819 a kuhina nui, an office combining the roles of regent and chief minister first held by Kamehameha’s widow <b>Kaʻahumanu</b>, shared power with the king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1819 <b>Kaʻahumanu</b>, Kamehameha’s favourite wife, became kuhina nui, co-ruler with his young son Liholiho.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li></ul></section>
